{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Give ear, O heavens.</b> Moses said האזנה to the heavens and השמעה to the earth, and Isaiah said the reverse — שמעו שמים והאזיני ארץ — because Moses' craft was in heaven, and it is the way to say to one who is with him 'האזן הסכת,' and to the earth, because it is far from him, he said שמיעה. And Isaiah, to the earth, because it was near him, used the language of האזיני — one who is with him needs only to incline his ear to hear, and because it is far he needs השמעה in a loud voice. And Rashi explained that he changed the terms because heaven and earth are witnesses, as it is written העידותי בכם היום את השמים ואת הארץ, and witnesses — when their testimony matches, their testimony stands. And had Isaiah not come and given שמיעה to the heavens and האזנה to the earth, the heavens would testify and say: 'When we were called to testify in the days of Moses with האזנה we heard,' and the earth testifies: 'I was called with the language of שמיעה' — and their testimony would not match. Isaiah came and switched the terms, so both are in the language of האזנה and both in the language of שמיעה. And Rav Huna said in the name of Rav Acha: from the words of both of them we learn that the upper ones are more numerous than the lower ones — for Moses said to the heavens האזינו in plural language, and to the earth ותשמע in singular language; and likewise Isaiah said to the heavens שמעו and to the earth והאזיני — singular language. And perhaps this is why the upper ones are called שמים in plural language and the lower ones ארץ in singular language."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:42.318089461Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}